Z||000011||ur2biteme||12-30-2000||05:23 AM||||if he wanted a camaro, he'd have said so.  mustangs are good cars, had too many trouble free mustangs in the family to say otherwise.  with that argument aside, the other comes into play.  the numbers on a camaro vs. mustang in a straight line.  well, when youre aftermarket on the car, do stock numbers really matter?  besides, if memory serves me correctly, mustangs originally found sucess on road courses, drag racing came later.  i just find the hazing from one side to the other to be annoying.  kinda like all the ricers telling me to get rid of my "primitive american car" (fiero) and get a (insert japanese car make here).  or the guy at the shop that we all have come to know and love that only can work on a front engine, rwd car with a 350.  does it really matter in the long run?  <P><P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>170k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000012||BBBAD GT||12-30-2000||03:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>351 clevelands a drop in for a 302, dont use a windsor. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think you have that backwards, the windsor is what you want inplace of the 302, if you are going that route.  You'll almost never find a cleveland in a late model mustang.<P>My advice is to just buy a Stang that already has the go fast goodies, because you'll be in no man's land if you try to start engine swapping a four cyclinder without knowing the ins and outs of the late model Stang.<P>I personally have a sportsman 351 windsor shortblock still in the crate from Ford Motorsport in the garage, waiting for the stock 302 to split.  This swap alone (even though it basically just bolts in) will be costly because of the stuff that needs to be replaced/upgraded that aren't compatiable.  But, its a much stronger block able to withstand 600rwhp easily, instead of the 450-500rwhp of the stock weak 302 block.<P>Always remember, there is <B>always</B> somebody faster than you.<P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">     <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000013||JohnnyK||12-31-2000||01:02 PM||||Ya ya.. There is always somebody better looking, smarter, etc..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I would just like a DAM* fast car..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I was going to get a 5.0 and put a 5.7 in it instead. Not a 4cyl to 8 cyl. A car with the go fast goodies? But thats costly, and I wanted to do some good work..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Kind of want a late 60's fastback though.. Anyone have any experience with those? How are they speedwise?||||reg||1
